#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SESSION_NOTES-S227-through-S225.md.verify.sh — the losslessness proof for this shard.
#
# Generated by Session 231 (the FOURTH trim). Re-runnable, read-only, exits non-zero on failure.
#
#   bash <this>              prove the fourth trim was lossless AND landed where it was declared
#   bash <this> --self-test  prove this proof can FAIL (50 mutants; exit 2 if any survives)
#
# Descended from Session 228's SESSION_NOTES-S224-through-S221.md.verify.sh, itself descended from
# Session 224's and Session 222's — read those for the rationale behind L0-L3, L4 and L5-L7. Shards
# are WRITE-ONCE: this proof pins ITS OWN cut key and does not re-prove the earlier shards' cuts.
# Run all four.
#
# TWO ASSERTIONS ARE NEW HERE. Neither is speculative: each closes a hole that an earlier proof
# NAMED IN ITS OWN PROSE and then declined to check.
#
#   L8 (REACH) — the two copies of the shard set that live outside every earlier proof. The third
#      trim's pointer block says it in as many words: "Two further copies live outside every proof
#      — `README.md`'s repo map and the shard-naming rule in `docs/methodology/PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md`
#      — and a trim that leaves either one alone ships a lie." CLAUDE.md repeats it. Both were
#      correct and both were unenforced: L5 reads three copies (live pointer, shard banner,
#      CLAUDE.md) and stops. So a trim could archive records, update every machine-checked copy,
#      pass all eight assertions green, and leave README.md telling the next reader there are three
#      shards holding everything before Session 225.
#
#      THE LIST IN THAT SENTENCE WAS ITSELF INCOMPLETE, which is the more useful half of this
#      finding. Sweeping the repo for the CLASS rather than trusting the named instances turned up
#      a THIRD unenforced copy the third trim never mentioned: BACKLOG.md's read-cap item, whose
#      whole subject is the shard set and which said "there are now **three** unwatched shards".
#      L8 covers all three. A fifth trim should sweep again rather than trust this list either —
#      `git grep -l 'SESSION_NOTES-'` is the sweep, and it takes one second.
#
#      L8 checks each of the two files two ways, because either alone is weak. (a) HAND-DECLARED
#      required and forbidden text — this catches the stale COUNT WORD ("the three shards above",
#      "Three instances so far"), which no derived check can see, because a count word is prose and
#      not a filename. (b) DERIVED FROM ROUTING — the set of shard filenames each file names must
#      equal the set ROUTING names, which catches both a shard left out and a phantom or typo'd one
#      without restating the list a second time.
#
#      DELIBERATE SCOPE WIDENING, and it has the same consequence L5's did: README.md and
#      PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md must now be part of the trim commit, or this proof is red from the
#      first run. That is the point. And the same limit applies as to L5 — this reads them AT THE
#      TRIM COMMIT. It checks them as AUTHORED; it does not police later edits.
#
#   L9 (LINEAGE) — write-once, enforced for EVERY shard rather than only the newest. L7 was
#      introduced by the third trim as "the only enforcement `write-once` has ever had", and that
#      was true, but it guards exactly one file: the shard that shipped with it. The S216 and S220
#      shards have no L7 at all (their proofs predate it), and the S224 shard's L7 lives in the
#      S224 proof — which nothing obliges a session to run. Meanwhile every assertion here reads
#      git history, so truncating or deleting an ANCESTOR shard leaves this suite fully green.
#      Measured, not assumed: `mv` the S216 shard aside and, once this trim is committed, L0-L8 all
#      pass. L9 compares each ancestor shard on disk against the blob committed at the commit that
#      ADDED it — its own commit, never this trim's — so it is applicable from the first run,
#      before this trim is even committed, unlike L7.
#
#      L9 IS ASYMMETRIC IN THE SAME WAY L7 IS, and for the same reason: shards only. The live
#      ledger is SUPPOSED to change and is never compared this way. L9 says nothing about the
#      earlier proofs' own cuts — it says their ARTIFACTS are still the bytes those proofs were
#      written about, which is a precondition for running them at all.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE, so the next author does not have to re-derive it, and so nobody mistakes ten
# assertions for ten independent opinions. Neuter one at a time and re-run --self-test; what then
# survives is what that assertion alone was catching:
#
#     for A in L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 L5 L6 L7 L8 L9; do sed "s|^    fails += $A(.*|    pass|" <this> > /tmp/n.sh
#       echo "$A: $(bash /tmp/n.sh --self-test | grep SURVIVED)"; done
#
#     L0 (none)  L1 (none)  L2 M10,M12-M15,M40  L3 (none)  L4 (none)  L5 M18-M26,M38,M39,M47
#     L6 M27,M28  L7 M29,M30  L8 M31-M34,M41-M46,M49,M50  L9 M35-M37,M48
#
# THAT LOOP IS NOT ENOUGH, and the ancestor's L5/2 is the proof it is not. An assertion is a
# handful of separate `if`s; neutering the whole function cannot tell you that ONE of those `if`s
# has never fired under any mutation. It looks like protection and has never been shown to work.
# So neuter one `if` at a time as well (`for s in required:` -> `for s in ():`,
# `if got_names != want_names:` -> `if False:`, and so on). The mutant each of these arms needs,
# being the only one that arm alone catches:
#
#     L5/2 shape M47   L5/2 OVERLAP M39   L8/required M50   L8/forbidden M44   L8/set M45,M46
#     L8/unreadable M49   L9/no-add-commit M48   L2/b0 M14,M15
#
# SIX of those arms had no such mutant until an adversarial review of THIS file measured it — one
# day after this same session wrote that very rule into CLAUDE.md. Writing the rule is not obeying
# it. Two footnotes a reader will otherwise trip over: neutering L5/2's shape arm CRASHES the run
# rather than surviving (with two open-below clauses the arm below it compares None to an int),
# so read a traceback as 'caught', not 'passed'; and no GAP mutant can be uniquely-L5/2, because
# the session ids are dense, so any gap wide enough to matter also strands a real record and
# trips L5/4. M38 is kept anyway — a gap and an overlap are different reader-facing mistakes.
#
# Those are MEASURED, not predicted. The first draft of this line said "L2 M10-M15" and was wrong:
# M11 leaves the third trim's stale routing table standing in the live front matter, so L5/1 sees
# it too and L2 is not the only assertion holding it. Run the loop; do not reason about it.
#
# RUNNING THAT LOOP IS HOW M38 AND M39 CAME TO EXIST, and it is the reason they are appended out of
# order at the end of the list rather than filed among the other L5 mutants. The third trim shipped
# `L5/2` — the clause that requires ROUTING to partition the session-id space with no gap and no
# overlap — and NOT ONE of its 28 mutants could reach it: the only two that mutate ROUTING itself
# (its M22, M23) both happen to stay contiguous, so the gap/overlap arm never executed under any
# mutation. That is precisely the defect the third trim caught in the second trim's `L2/b0`, one
# level further down, in the very assertion it was adding. It is not a coincidence twice: an
# assertion added late gets a mutant; a SUB-CLAUSE added inside it usually does not. M39 is the
# uniquely-L5/2 mutant (its overlap satisfies L5/1, L5/3 and L5/4 and only the partition check
# objects). M38 is a gap, and no gap mutant can be uniquely-L5/2 — the session ids are dense, so
# any gap wide enough to matter also strands a real record and trips L5/4. Said plainly rather than
# left for the next reader to discover.
#
# The other four assertions are not individually load-bearing, and saying so is the point:
#
#   * L0, L1 and L3 overlap by construction and always have — L1 is the primary byte detector and
#     L3 is per-record naming layered on top of it, so a mutant falsifies both or neither.
#   * L4 stopped being uniquely load-bearing when the third trim added L5/4, which catches a
#     whole-record boundary shift too (M16 -> the shard would hold Session 228; M17 -> the live
#     file would hold 227). It is kept anyway: it states the cut key directly instead of inferring
#     it, its failure message names the retained and archived sides, and an assertion is not
#     deadweight merely because a later, broader one happens to subsume it.
#
# This suite proves L0/L1/L3/L4 are jointly SUFFICIENT with the rest, not individually NECESSARY.
#
# The grammar is FROZEN into this file on purpose: a later change to the trim design must not
# silently change what THIS shard's proof means.
set -euo pipefail
exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
